 go through the same path.\n  - It works under bash or zsh. The prefix runs after the snapshot and startup files, in the command's real workdir, inside the sandbox, and nothing leaks into child processes.\n  - The tested hook script is at `/tmp/claude-1000/-home-thom-src-github-com-tteggel--dotfiles/5177785d-cd1e-4fc5-a113-5f63d7df1406/scratchpad/codex-research/hook/codex-direnv-hook`. It uses jq, skips commands mentioning apply_patch or direnv, and is idempotent. It prepends:\n  ```sh\n  if command -v direnv >/dev/null; then _de=$(direnv export bash 2>/dev/null) || echo \"direnv: export failed (.envrc blocked?)\" >&2; eval \"$_de\"; unset _de; fi\n  [ -z \"${NIX_DIRENV_DID_FALLBACK-}\" ] || echo \"direnv: devshell stale (nix re-eval blocked by sandbox)\" >&2\n  ```\n  - Put it in `/etc/codex/config.toml`. Hooks there count as managed, so they run without trust review (`hooks/src/engine/discovery.rs:796-829`). In `~/.codex` or via `-c` they need re-trusting whenever the store path changes. The matcher is an unanchored regex, hence `^Bash$`.\n  ```toml\n  [[hooks.PreToolUse]]\n  matcher = \"^Bash$\"\n  hooks = [{ type = \"command\", command = \"<store>/bin/codex-direnv-hook\", timeout = 10 }]\n  ```\n  \n  Caveats:\n  - It adds about 0.15 s per command: roughly 0.13 s for `direnv export` with a fresh cache, plus about 10 ms for the hook.\n  - The prefix is visible in the TUI, approval prompts, execpolicy prefix rules and PostToolUse hooks.\n  - apply_patch heredocs must not be wrapped, because Codex only intercepts them as the sole statement (`apply-patch/src/invocation.rs:257-290`).\n  - Your own `!` commands and MCP servers aren't covered.\n  - A blocked `.envrc` exports nothing; the prefix prints a warning.\n  \n  The shell-level alternative is a `BASH_ENV` exported by the wrapper, not `~/.zshenv`. It leaks into every child bash, including direnv's own (call it as `BASH_ENV= direnv export` to avoid recursion). It also only reaches commands when Codex runs its embedded server rather than the shared daemon. Your `-c` flags already rule out the daemon (`tui/src/daemon_startup.rs:94-119`); `--no-daemon` makes that explicit (#24638).\n  \n  **Sandbox results** (`codex sandbox -P :workspace -C ~/src/github.com/tteggel/ezal`; direnv status shows ezal is allowed)\n  - **Up-to-date cache:** `direnv export zsh` and `direnv export json` work: exit 0, the same 85 variables as outside the sandbox. nix-direnv makes no nix calls and only `touch -h`es `.direnv`, which is writable.\n  - **Re-evaluation needed:** I forced this with a scratch `direnv_layout_dir`, without touching the project.\n    - It first fails on the read-only `~/.cache/nix` (\"unable to open database file\").\n    - With the cache redirected to a writable dir, it fails on `cannot connect to socket at '/nix/var/nix/daemon-socket/socket': Operation not permitted`. In restricted-network mode seccomp blocks every `connect()` (`linux-sandbox/src/seccomp.rs:203-232`).\n    - In both cases nix-direnv falls back to the previous environment, sets `NIX_DIRENV_DID_FALLBACK=1` and exits 0, so the command silently runs with the stale devshell. I reproduced this with a stale copy of the cache, and the prefix's warning fired.\n  - **Network enabled:** with `sandbox_workspace_write.network_access=true` the daemon is reachable, and adding a writable cache made `nix print-dev-env .` succeed in 3.7 s. Linux has no per-socket allowlist (`--allow-unix-socket` is macOS-only). You are a trusted Nix user (`Trusted: 1`), so giving the sandbox daemon access is close to a sandbox escape. Having the model run an escalated `direnv reload` is safer.\n  \n  **Docs, issues and PRs**\n  - Neither the repo docs nor the official hooks page mention direnv or Nix.\n  - Open issues: #4843 (login bash drops per-project env), #25452 (first-class project command environments), #24638 (daemon env differs), #49992 (bash snapshot skips `.bashrc` when `BASH_ENV` is set).\n  - #11223 (snapshot breaks direnv) was closed after PR #11231 tied snapshots to the cwd. PR #26715 (load direnv into snapshots) was closed without merging.\n  \n  **Verified:** shell and argv from your session logs and running processes, `codex features list`, startup-file order, every sandbox run, and the hook's rewritten command under bash and zsh in the sandbox.\n  \n  **Inferred from source and docs only:** Codex applying the hook in a live session, `/etc` hooks running without trust review, and using the real `~/.cache/nix` as a writable root (I only tested a redirected cache).\n  \n  **Side effects:** nix-direnv updated timestamps under `ezal/.direnv`. `nix flake archive` on the dotfiles did not touch `flake.lock`. Everything else went to the scratchpad.","handback":true,"handbackReportText":"Codex runs every model command through bash on this machine, not zsh, so a `~/.zshenv` hook would never fire. The native fix is a PreToolUse hook that rewrites each command to run `direnv export` first. I tested the rewritten command inside the sandbox and it loads the devshell. I didn't run a model, so the hook firing in a live session is inferred from source and docs. Source paths below are under `codex-rs/` at rust-v0.163.0.\n\n**Why bash, and the exact argv**\n- Codex decides the shell from the basename of the passwd shell (`shell-command/src/shell_detect.rs:39-59,359-383`), and there is no config override (`core/src/session/session.rs:1413-1416`).\n- NixOS-WSL always sets the passwd shell to `…-wrapped-zsh/wrapper` (nixos-wsl `modules/systemd/native/wrap-shell.nix`). \"wrapper\" isn't recognised, so on Linux Codex falls back to `which bash`.\n- Your session logs confirm it: `<shell>bash</shell>` and argv `[\"/sbin/bash\",\"-lc\",cmd]`. In 10 recent sessions it was a devshell `bash-5.3p15` instead, because codex inherited a direnv PATH (a running codex process has `DIRENV_DIR` and `IN_NIX_SHELL` set).\n- The argv is built in `core/src/shell.rs:24-33`; login is on by default (`core/src/tools/handlers/unified_exec.rs:97-123`). There are two shapes:\n  - **Workdir equals the session cwd and a snapshot exists:** `[bash,\"-c\",\"<save CODEX_*>; if . '<snap>' >/dev/null 2>&1; then :; fi; <re-export>; exec bash -c '<cmd>'\"]` (`core/src/tools/runtimes/mod.rs:285-517`, condition at `core/src/session/turn_context.rs:140-148`). Only `BASH_ENV` is read, once in the outer shell and again in the inner one. Under zsh it would be `.zshenv`, twice.\n  - **Any other workdir:** plain `bash -lc`. That reads `/etc/profile`, then `~/.bash_profile`, then `BASH_ENV`. Under zsh it would be `.zshenv`, `.zprofile`, `.zlogin`, never `.zshrc`.\n- I confirmed both startup orders with a scratch HOME and ZDOTDIR.\n\n**Snapshot behaviour**\n- `codex features list` shows `shell_snapshot` on and `shell_snapshot_v2` off (`features/src/lib.rs:1096-1121`).\n- It is captured once per session, in the session cwd, by a login shell that also sources `~/.bashrc` or `~/.zshrc` (`shell-command/src/startup.rs`). It dumps functions, options, aliases and every export except PWD/OLDPWD.\n- Sourcing it replaces PATH. So PATH edits made by the outer shell's startup files are lost, and edits made by the inner shell's startup files win (checked empirically).\n- Your 59-line snapshot files have no exports at all: the devshell bash lacks `compgen`, so those sessions restored no environment.\n\n**How the command environment is built**\n- It is Codex's own environment filtered by `shell_environment_policy` (`protocol/src/shell_environment.rs:105-175`). That includes everything by default; the KEY/SECRET/TOKEN default excludes are off unless you set `ignore_default_excludes = false`.\n- Codex adds `CODEX_*` variables and forces `NO_COLOR=1`, `TERM=dumb`, `LANG=C.UTF-8` and `PAGER=cat` (`core/src/unified_exec/process_manager.rs:94-105`).\n- `experimental_use_profile` is parsed but never read, so it does nothing.\n- No hook can set environment variables: SessionStart only returns additional context, PreToolUse only allow-with-rewrite or deny.\n\n**Recommendation: PreToolUse command rewrite**\n- A hook returning `permissionDecision:\"allow\"` with `updatedInput.command` rewrites the `exec_command` call (`hooks/src/engine/output_parser.rs:121-182`, `core/src/tools/registry.rs:702-743`). Code-mode `tools.exec_command` calls go through the same path.\n- It works under bash or zsh. The prefix runs after the snapshot and startup files, in the command's real workdir, inside the sandbox, and nothing leaks into child processes.\n- The tested hook script is at `/tmp/claude-1000/-home-thom-src-github-com-tteggel--dotfiles/5177785d-cd1e-4fc5-a113-5f63d7df1406/scratchpad/codex-research/hook/codex-direnv-hook`. It uses jq, skips commands mentioning apply_patch or direnv, and is idempotent. It prepends:\n```sh\nif command -v direnv >/dev/null; then _de=$(direnv export bash 2>/dev/null) || echo \"direnv: export failed (.envrc blocked?)\" >&2; eval \"$_de\"; unset _de; fi\n[ -z \"${NIX_DIRENV_DID_FALLBACK-}\" ] || echo \"direnv: devshell stale (nix re-eval blocked by sandbox)\" >&2\n```\n- Put it in `/etc/codex/config.toml`. Hooks there count as managed, so they run without trust review (`hooks/src/engine/discovery.rs:796-829`). In `~/.codex` or via `-c` they need re-trusting whenever the store path changes. The matcher is an unanchored regex, hence `^Bash$`.\n```toml\n[[hooks.PreToolUse]]\nmatcher = \"^Bash$\"\nhooks = [{ type = \"command\", command = \"<store>/bin/codex-direnv-hook\", timeout = 10 }]\n```\n\nCaveats:\n- It adds about 0.15 s per command: roughly 0.13 s for `direnv export` with a fresh cache, plus about 10 ms for the hook.\n- The prefix is visible in the TUI, approval prompts, execpolicy prefix rules and PostToolUse hooks.\n- apply_patch heredocs must not be wrapped, because Codex only intercepts them as the sole statement (`apply-patch/src/invocation.rs:257-290`).\n- Your own `!` commands and MCP servers aren't covered.\n- A blocked `.envrc` exports nothing; the prefix prints a warning.\n